#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Convert a mini4pro flight record into a DiMOS **mem2** store, offline.

    tools/memexport datasets/2026-07-27-apriltag-nadir-25hz/20260727-192417.001.jsonl
    tools/memexport session.001.jsonl --out tmp/flight.db
    tools/memexport session.001.jsonl --keyframes-only     # 0.5% of frames, 5% of bytes
    tools/memexport --spy tmp/flight.db                    # what a consumer will see
    tools/memexport --selftest                             # planted data, planted faults

Streams: pose, odom, gps_location, imu, battery, mode, tf, color_image, camera_info,
detections, tag_fix, setpoint, wind, status, health, command_ack, provenance — each created
even when empty, so "none occurred" and "this converter cannot produce them" stay
distinguishable.

- **One recording, one flight, one store.** Local coordinates are metres from *this* flight's
  takeoff point, so a store holding two would silently place one somewhere it never was. Refused.
- **The video is copied, never decoded.** `--keyframes-only` is therefore the only decimation on
  offer; any other rate would store frames that decode to garbage.
- **`camera_info` is a fitted focal length, not a calibration**, and says so in its own tags.
- **Four cross-checks run on every conversion and abort it on mismatch**: the Kotlin encoder byte
  for byte, a round trip out of SQLite, the origin by subtraction, video conformance.
  `--no-crosscheck` skips them, loudly.
- Re-execs into DiMOS's venv, the only interpreter with `dimos.memory2` and the LCM bindings.

Full rationale, and the specification this implements: `docs/mem2-converter.md`.
"""

